        {
          "question": "window-near-endpoint",
          "status": "answered",
          "node": "a97A02:B1",
          "quote": "The reading is the zero\nextension: integrate against Lebesgue measure restricted to \\( [0,1] \\), so\nthe part of the window outside contributes nothing, which is exactly the\nintegral of the extended function, and the average is the window integral\ndivided by \\( 2h \\). Near an endpoint you are averaging over a shorter\neffective window, still dividing by \\( 2h \\).",
          "ink_words": 62,
          "graph_support": [
            {
              "graph": "holes/inkup/reasoning/a97A02-window-meaning.json",
              "edge": {
                "from": "restriction-warrant",
                "relation": "warrants",
                "to": "window-meaning"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "question": "continuity-from-integrability",
          "status": "answered",
          "node": "a97A02:B4",
          "quote": "Since \\( f \\) is\nintegrable, as in B2, \\( F \\) is continuous: for \\( b < c \\),\n\\[ F(c) - F(b) = \\int_{(b,c]} f , \\]\nand by absolute continuity of the integral this is small whenever the\ninterval \\( (b,c] \\) has small measure. Hence \\( t \\mapsto F(t\\pm h) \\) is\ncontinuous on \\( [0,1] \\).",
          "ink_words": 61,
          "graph_support": [
            {
              "graph": "holes/inkup/reasoning/a97A02-primitive-continuity.json",
              "edge": {
                "from": "primitive-warrant",
                "relation": "requires",
                "to": "integrable-f"
              }
            },
            {
              "graph": "holes/inkup/reasoning/a97A02-primitive-continuity.json",
              "edge": {
                "from": "primitive-warrant",
                "relation": "requires",
                "to": "no-atoms"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "question": "why-not-symmetric-difference",
          "status": "answered",
          "node": "a97A02:B4",
          "quote": "For \\( p > 1 \\) that works, with care: the difference has\nmeasure \\( 2\\min(\\lvert s-t\\rvert, 2h) \\), not the \\( 2\\lvert s-t\\rvert \\) one\nfirst writes down, since once \\( \\lvert s-t\\rvert \\) exceeds \\( 2h \\) the\nwindows are disjoint. At \\( p = 1 \\) the Hölder estimate gives no decay, and\none falls back on absolute continuity of the integral. That fallback alone\nproves continuity for every \\( p \\ge 1 \\), so we use it from the start",
          "ink_words": 84,
          "graph_support": [
            {
              "graph": "holes/inkup/reasoning/a97A02-why-primitives.json",
              "edge": {
                "from": "sd-bookkeeping",
                "relation": "resolved-by",
                "to": "primitive-move"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "question": "bound-needs-no-t-restriction",
          "status": "answered",
          "node": "a97A02:B7",
          "quote": "Notice which\nhypotheses the chain consumed: measurability, the window bound, and\n\\( p \\ge 1 \\). The restriction \\( t \\in [0,1] \\) never entered, so the same\nestimate holds for every real \\( t \\).",
          "ink_words": 36,
          "graph_support": [
            {
              "graph": "holes/inkup/reasoning/a97A02-bound-all-real-t.json",
              "edge": {
                "from": "holder-window-warrant",
                "relation": "warrants",
                "to": "all-real-t-bound"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "question": "why-this-constant",
          "status": "answered",
          "node": "a97A02:B6",
          "quote": "The constant that will\nsurvive is not arbitrary: \\( (2h)^{-1/p} \\) is the \\( L^{q} \\) norm of the\nnormalised window indicator \\( (2h)^{-1}\\mathbf{1}_{[t-h,t+h]} \\), a constant\n\\( (2h)^{-1} \\) on a set of measure \\( 2h \\), giving\n\\( (2h)^{-1}(2h)^{1/q} = (2h)^{-1/p} \\), with \\( 1/q = 0 \\) when \\( p = 1 \\).",
          "ink_words": 56,
